%% CASE Sentinel flight simulator programme
Sentinel Systems is the prime contractor delivering flight training simulators to a national air force. The hardware (cockpit replicas and motion platforms) follows a predictive plan with formal design reviews; agile teams develop the simulation software.

- The contract requires everyone working with **classified flight data** to hold a security clearance, and the data may be processed **only on accredited systems** at Sentinel's secure site.
- The manufacturer of a key **graphics processor** used in the visual system will **discontinue it in 18 months**. The simulators must be supported for 15 years.
- Instructors who currently train pilots in real aircraft doubt that simulators can replace real flying hours.
- A new government has announced a **defence spending review** that could cut the programme's budget by 15%.
@@ BE2 | Predictive
To meet a deadline, a cleared developer wants to copy classified flight data to a laptop and work from home over the weekend. What should the project manager do?
A) Refuse, and find another way, such as planned weekend work at the secure site
B) Allow it this once, since the developer holds a valid security clearance for the classified data
C) Allow it, provided that the developer encrypts the laptop and returns the data on Monday morning
D) Ask the customer's security officer for permission after the work has been completed successfully
= A
? Classified data may be processed only on accredited systems. A clearance does not override this rule, and asking for permission afterwards would be a security breach.

@@ BE5 | Hybrid
How should the project respond to the discontinuation of the graphics processor?
A) Plan a response now, such as a last-time buy or qualifying an alternative part
B) Wait until the processor has been discontinued before deciding what to do
C) Ignore it, since the simulators will be delivered before the processor is discontinued
D) Ask the manufacturer to keep producing the processor for the next fifteen years
= A
? Component obsolescence is a known risk. Options such as a last-time buy or an alternative part are analysed and decided early, considering the 15-year support obligation.

@@ BE7 | Hybrid
How should the project address the instructors' doubts about the simulators?
A) Involve instructors in design reviews and trials, and train them to teach with simulators
B) Ask the air force's commanders to order the instructors to use the simulators as planned
C) Leave the instructors alone, since they will accept the simulators once they are delivered
D) Replace the sceptical instructors with new instructors who have no previous expectations
= A
? Involving the people affected lets them shape the solution and see its value. Training builds their ability to teach in the new way.

@@ BE8 | Predictive
How should the project respond to the announced spending review?
A) Prepare options with the customer, such as phasing deliveries or prioritising capabilities
B) Continue as planned, since the contract has been signed and the budget cannot legally be changed
C) Stop all work until the outcome of the spending review is known, to avoid wasting any money
D) Lobby the new government to exclude the programme from the review, since it is important
= A
? External changes are anticipated. Preparing options with the customer allows a fast, informed decision once the review's outcome is known.
%% END CASE

@@ BE1 | Predictive
What is the main purpose of an independent assurance review at key stages of a major programme?
A) To give decision-makers an objective view of the programme's health and readiness
B) To replace the steering committee's decisions with the decisions of external experts
C) To check the timesheets of every member of the programme team for accuracy
D) To find out which team members are responsible for the programme's problems
= A
? Independent assurance provides an objective assessment that supports governance decisions at critical points. It does not replace the decision-makers.

@@ BE1 | Predictive
Who is usually accountable for the project's business case?
A) The sponsor
B) The project manager
C) The PMO
D) The project team
= A
? The sponsor owns the business case and is accountable for the project delivering value. The project manager helps keep it up to date.

@@ BE1 | Hybrid
The project manager wants to sign a $250,000 purchase order, but the organisation's delegation of authority allows project managers to sign up to $100,000. What should the project manager do?
A) Get the purchase approved by someone with the required authority
B) Split the order into three smaller orders, each under the $100,000 limit
C) Sign the order, since the purchase is already included in the approved budget
D) Ask the supplier to start work while the approval process is under way
= A
? Project governance operates within the organisation's delegated authority. Splitting orders to avoid limits breaches policy and ethics.

@@ BE1 | Agile
In Scrum, who decides whether to release an increment to customers?
A) The product owner, within the organisation's release policies
B) The scrum master, after checking the team's velocity and quality
C) The developers, by majority vote at the end of the sprint review
D) The PMO, after a formal stage-gate review
= A
? The product owner is accountable for maximising value, which includes deciding when to release, within the organisation's policies and controls.

@@ BE1 | Hybrid
The railway's new signalling system cannot enter service until the safety regulator accepts the safety case. How should the governance framework reflect this?
A) As a formal gate, with the safety case planned as a key deliverable
B) As an informal check that the team performs just before go-live
C) As a risk, since the regulator may or may not ask for a safety case at the end
D) As the regulator's responsibility, outside the project's governance
= A
? External approvals that control progress are built into governance as decision gates, with the deliverables they require planned from the start.

@@ BE1 | Hybrid
Several teams use farm data in different ways, and nobody knows who may approve new uses of the data. What should be established?
A) Data governance with clear data owners and approval rules
B) A rule that every team may use the data as it sees fit for its own purposes
C) A ban on any new uses of the farm data from now on
D) Approval of every data use by the chief executive
= A
? Data governance defines ownership, decision rights and rules for using data, which protects farmers' interests and enables responsible use.

@@ BE2 | Hybrid
Safety-critical signalling software must be verified by people who are independent of its developers. How should the project comply?
A) Plan independent verification and validation in the schedule and organisation
B) Ask the developers to review each other's code to save time and money
C) Let the safety regulator perform the verification after delivery of the system
D) Skip independent verification, since the developers are highly experienced
= A
? Safety standards often require independent verification. It must be planned with suitably independent people and enough time in the schedule.

@@ BE2 | Hybrid
The agritech team wants to fly drones beyond the pilots' line of sight to survey large farms. This requires a special permit. What should the project do?
A) Apply for the permit early and trial within the current rules meanwhile
B) Start the flights now, since the permit is very likely to be granted in the end
C) Ask the farmers to fly the drones, since flights over their own land need no permit
D) Drop drone surveys entirely, since the permit process is too slow and uncertain
= A
? Regulatory permissions are planned for early, and work continues within the rules that already apply. Flying without a permit would be illegal.

@@ BE2 | Agile
The defence contract requires every software change to be traceable to an approved requirement. How can the agile teams comply efficiently?
A) Link backlog items and code changes to requirement IDs as part of the definition of done
B) Produce a traceability report by hand at the end of the contract, once all the work is done
C) Ask the customer to waive the traceability requirement for the agile part of the work
D) Trace only the most important changes, since tracing every change would slow the teams
= A
? Building traceability into everyday tools and the definition of done meets the requirement continuously, without a large manual effort at the end.

@@ BE2 | Predictive
A consulting firm is asked to redesign a client's finance processes. Another part of the same firm audits the client's financial statements. What should be checked before accepting the work?
A) Auditor independence rules and any conflict of interest
B) Whether the client is willing to pay a higher fee for the additional consulting work
C) Whether the audit team agrees to share its findings with the consulting team
D) Nothing, since the two teams belong to different departments of the firm
= A
? Auditor independence rules restrict the other services that an audit firm may provide to its audit clients. Conflicts of interest must be checked before work is accepted.

@@ BE2 | Predictive
A new version of a safety standard is published during the project, but the contract refers to the previous version. What should the project manager do?
A) Assess the differences and agree the applicable version with the customer formally
B) Switch to the new version immediately, since newer standards are always mandatory
C) Ignore the new version, since only the contract's version is legally binding
D) Apply the new version to half of the system to compare the two approaches
= A
? A change in an applicable standard is analysed for impact and agreed with the customer formally, since it may affect scope, cost and schedule.

@@ BE3 | Predictive
The change control board defers a decision on a change request because the impact analysis is incomplete. What should the project manager do?
A) Complete the analysis, resubmit it and keep the change log updated
B) Implement the change anyway, since the board did not reject the change request
C) Close the request, since a deferred change will never be approved by the board
D) Ask the requester to submit the change again under a different name
= A
? Deferred requests remain open. The missing analysis is completed so that the board can decide, and the change log reflects the current status.

@@ BE3 | Predictive
A design change to the simulator's cockpit hardware is approved. Which items must also be updated?
A) All affected configuration items, including manuals and training materials
B) Only the drawings, since the change affects only the cockpit hardware itself
C) Only the schedule, since the design has already been approved
D) Nothing else, since the change control board has already approved it
= A
? Configuration management ensures that every item affected by a change, including documentation and training materials, is updated consistently.

@@ BE3 | Hybrid
Halfway through a fixed-fee consulting engagement, the client asks the consultants to swap one business unit in the analysis for another of similar size. What should happen?
A) Assess the impact and document the agreed swap formally
B) Make the swap informally, since the work involved is about the same size
C) Refuse the swap, since the scope of a fixed-fee engagement cannot change
D) Charge an additional fee, since every change to the scope must cost more
= A
? Even changes with no cost impact are assessed and documented, so that the agreed scope stays clear and future disputes are avoided.

@@ BE3 | Agile
The team wants to change its sprint length from two weeks to three. How should this be handled?
A) Decide it with the product owner in a retrospective, then keep the new length consistent
B) Change the length every sprint, depending on how much work is planned for the sprint
C) Ask the PMO to approve the change through the organisation's formal change control process
D) Keep the two-week length, since sprint length can never change once it has been chosen
= A
? Teams can adapt their process. A change in sprint length is agreed with the product owner and then kept stable, so that the cadence remains predictable.

@@ BE3 | Hybrid
Two weeks before the new timetable system goes live, the project manager wants to stop all non-critical changes. What is this called, and why is it done?
A) A change freeze, to stabilise the system and reduce release risk
B) A change backlog, to collect requests for the system's next version
C) A scope reduction, to lower the cost of the remaining work
D) A rolling wave, to plan the remaining work in more detail
= A
? A change freeze limits changes to critical fixes before a major release, which reduces the risk of new defects at go-live.

@@ BE3 | Hybrid
The team wants to update the firmware of 8,000 sensors already installed on farms. How should this change be released?
A) In controlled stages, with testing and a rollback plan, following the change process
B) To all sensors at once, so that every farm benefits from the update on the same day
C) Only when a farmer reports a problem with their sensors to the support team
D) Without testing, since the update fixes only a small number of known issues
= A
? Changes to systems in use are released in controlled stages, monitored and reversible, to limit the impact of any problems.

@@ BE4 | Predictive | multi
The customer is three weeks late in providing interface data that the contract requires it to supply. Which two actions are appropriate? (Choose two.)
A) Record it as an issue and assess the impact
B) Notify the customer formally, as the contract requires
C) Stop all work until the data arrives
D) Create the data based on the team's assumptions
E) Leave the delay out of status reports
= A, B
? Late customer-furnished information is an issue that may affect the schedule and the contract. It is recorded and analysed, and the customer is notified formally, which protects the project's position.

@@ BE4 | Agile
Stories are often blocked because subject matter experts from operations are rarely available to answer questions. What should the scrum master do?
A) Agree regular availability of the experts with their manager
B) Let the developers make assumptions and fix any mistakes later
C) Remove the stories that need experts from the product backlog
D) Ask the product owner to answer all of the operational questions
= A
? Missing access to experts is an impediment. The scrum master works with the experts' management to secure the time the team needs.

@@ BE4 | Hybrid
During a track closure, the work is running late and may not finish before trains must run again. What should the project manager do?
A) Follow the agreed contingency plan, such as stopping at a safe point and handing back the track
B) Keep working until the job is finished, even if this means delaying the first trains of the day
C) Hand back the track on time, whatever state the work and the equipment are in at that point
D) Ask the train operator to cancel the morning services so that the work can be completed in full
= A
? Overrun risks on closures are planned for in advance. The contingency plan defines safe stopping points so that the railway can reopen safely and on time.

@@ BE4 | Agile
Builds fail at random because some automated tests are unreliable, and developers have started to ignore build failures. What should the team do?
A) Treat it as an impediment and fix or isolate the unreliable tests first
B) Ignore the failures, since most of them are caused by the tests
C) Delete all automated tests and return to manual testing for now
D) Run the build several times until it passes, and then release the increment as normal
= A
? Unreliable tests destroy trust in the build and hide real defects. Fixing or isolating them is a priority impediment.

@@ BE4 | Hybrid
The consultants are waiting for access to the client's data because the client's IT security approvals are slow. What should the engagement manager do?
A) Escalate through the client's sponsor and agree interim work, such as using anonymised samples
B) Ask the client's staff to send the data by personal email, so that work can start straight away
C) Wait until access is granted, and extend the engagement's end date by the time that is lost
D) Start the analysis using assumptions, and replace them with real data once it is available
= A
? The impediment is escalated through the right channel, while work continues in a compliant way. Bypassing security controls is not acceptable.

@@ BE4 | Predictive
Resolving an issue requires a change to the approved scope. What should the project manager do?
A) Raise a change request through change control
B) Change the scope directly, since the issue is urgent
C) Record the issue as closed and move on
D) Ask the team to resolve it within the current scope
= A
? When an issue's resolution affects a baseline, a change request is raised and decided through change control.

@@ BE5 | Predictive | fill
Option 1 costs $200,000, with a 30% chance of an extra $50,000. Option 2 costs $150,000, with a 40% chance of an extra $150,000. What is the expected cost of the cheaper option? (Answer in dollars.)
= $210,000
? Option 1: 200,000 + 0.30 × 50,000 = $215,000. Option 2: 150,000 + 0.40 × 150,000 = $210,000. Option 2 has the lower expected cost, $210,000.

@@ BE5 | Agile
How does delivering in small increments reduce risk?
A) Problems and wrong assumptions are found early, while they are cheap to correct
B) It removes the need for risk management, since increments are always small
C) It allows the team to postpone testing until the final increment has been built
D) It makes the final delivery date certain, since each increment is short and fully planned
= A
? Frequent delivery and feedback expose technical and business risks early, when changing direction is still cheap.

@@ BE5 | Predictive
Risk A has a probability of 0.3 and an impact score of 0.8. Risk B has a probability of 0.7 and an impact score of 0.2. Which risk has the higher risk score?
A) Risk A, with a score of 0.24
B) Risk B, with a score of 0.14
C) Risk B, with a score of 0.90
D) Both have the same score
= A
? Risk score = probability × impact. Risk A: 0.3 × 0.8 = 0.24. Risk B: 0.7 × 0.2 = 0.14. Risk A is higher.

@@ BE5 | Hybrid
The customer accepts schedule risk much more readily than safety risk. How should this affect the project's risk responses?
A) Responses should prioritise reducing safety risk, even at some cost to the schedule
B) All risks should receive the same type of response, whatever the customer's priorities are
C) Schedule risks should be eliminated first, since delays are visible to the customer
D) Safety risks can be accepted, since the customer has a high tolerance for risk
= A
? Risk responses reflect stakeholders' risk appetite. Where safety tolerance is low, safety risks receive stronger responses, even at a schedule cost.

@@ BE5 | Predictive
Several low-priority risks do not need active responses at present. What should be done with them?
A) Keep them on a watch list and review them periodically
B) Delete them from the risk register to keep it short
C) Assign a full response plan to each one immediately
D) Transfer them all to an insurer to be on the safe side
= A
? Low-priority risks are placed on a watch list and monitored, so that action can be taken if their probability or impact changes.

@@ BE5 | Agile
The business case assumes that farmers will be willing to share their field data with the company. What should the team do first?
A) Test this riskiest assumption early with a small experiment
B) Build the full platform and then check whether farmers share data
C) Ask the sales team whether they believe farmers will share their data
D) Record the assumption in the plan and review it at the end of the project
= A
? Testing the riskiest assumption first reduces the chance of building something that will fail, at low cost.

@@ BE6 | Agile
Work items spend two days in active work and eight days waiting in queues. What is the flow efficiency?
A) 20%
B) 25%
C) 80%
D) 10%
= A
? Flow efficiency = active time / total time = 2 / (2 + 8) = 20%. Reducing waiting time is the main way to improve it.

@@ BE6 | Predictive
A defence contractor wants to assess and improve the maturity of its development processes against a recognised model. Which model is commonly used for this?
A) CMMI (Capability Maturity Model Integration)
B) The Tuckman model of team development
C) The Kano model of customer satisfaction
D) The salience model of stakeholders
= A
? CMMI defines maturity levels and practices for improving development processes and is widely used in defence and government contracting.

@@ BE6 | Agile | multi
Releases happen once every three months and are risky and painful. Which two improvements would help most? (Choose two.)
A) Releasing smaller batches more frequently
B) Automating the build, test and deployment
C) Bundling more features into each release
D) Adding a longer manual test phase
E) Releasing only once a year instead
= A, B
? Smaller, more frequent releases with automation reduce the risk of each release and make releasing routine. Bigger, rarer releases make the problem worse.

@@ BE6 | Hybrid | match
Match each lean improvement practice with its purpose.
1) Gemba walk
2) Poka-yoke
3) Kaizen
4) Value stream mapping
A) Observing the work where it actually happens
B) Designing processes or parts so that mistakes cannot occur
C) Making small, continuous improvements
D) Visualising the flow of work and the waste across a process
= 1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D
? These lean practices help teams see problems, prevent errors and improve continuously.

@@ BE6 | Predictive
Which structured problem-solving method from lean summarises the problem, its root causes and the countermeasures on a single sheet of paper?
A) A3 problem solving
B) Earned value analysis
C) Critical path analysis
D) Monte Carlo simulation
= A
? A3 problem solving documents the background, current state, root cause analysis, countermeasures and follow-up on one A3-sized page.

@@ BE7 | Predictive
Before the new rail operations process is introduced, the project manager wants to know how each role's daily work will change. What should be carried out?
A) A change impact assessment by role
B) A quality audit of the new process
C) A procurement audit of suppliers
D) A Monte Carlo schedule analysis
= A
? A change impact assessment identifies how each group's work will change, which guides communication, training and support.

@@ BE7 | Hybrid
In a hierarchical defence organisation, junior engineers stay silent in agile events whenever senior officers are present. What should the agile coach do?
A) Create safe ways for everyone to contribute, and coach leaders to invite challenge
B) Ask the senior officers to stop attending all agile events from now on
C) Accept the silence, since the organisation's hierarchy cannot be changed by the project
D) Hold separate events for junior engineers and for senior officers
= A
? Cultural change is supported by structures that make it safe to speak up and by leaders who actively invite challenge.

@@ BE7 | Hybrid
Who should lead the organisational change when a client implements a consulting firm's recommendations?
A) The client's own leaders, supported by the consultants
B) The consultants, since they wrote the recommendations
C) The client's human resources department alone
D) Nobody, since good recommendations implement themselves
= A
? Change sticks when the organisation's own leaders own it. Consultants can support, but they cannot sustain the change after they leave.

@@ BE7 | Agile
Operations staff resist joining cross-functional product teams because they fear losing their expertise and status. What should be done?
A) Involve them in designing the new roles and show that their expertise is valued
B) Move them into the product teams quickly, so that resistance has no time to grow
C) Keep operations separate from the product teams to avoid the conflict completely
D) Tell them that their specialist roles will disappear whatever they think of it
= A
? Involving people in shaping their new roles and recognising their expertise addresses the fears behind resistance.

@@ BE7 | Predictive
Train drivers resist a new rostering system because it will reduce their overtime earnings. What should the project do?
A) Acknowledge the loss, engage the drivers and union, and seek fair transition terms
B) Tell the drivers that the system will not affect their earnings, so that resistance stops
C) Introduce the system without consultation, since rostering is a management decision
D) Delay the system indefinitely, since the drivers will never accept losing their overtime
= A
? Resistance can be based on real losses. Acknowledging them honestly and negotiating fair arrangements builds the support needed for change.

@@ BE8 | Agile
Major farm equipment makers adopt a new open standard for exchanging field data. What should the product owner do?
A) Assess its impact and value, and consider adding support for it to the roadmap
B) Ignore it, since the product already has its own data format that works well
C) Stop all other work and implement the standard in the next sprint
D) Wait for customers to ask for it before spending any time evaluating the new standard
= A
? Industry standards can change customer expectations and integration needs. The product owner evaluates them and adapts the roadmap if they add value.

@@ BE8 | Predictive
A national rail strike is announced for next month, during the planned commissioning tests. What should the project manager do?
A) Assess the impact, plan alternatives such as rescheduling the tests and inform stakeholders
B) Carry on with the tests as planned, since the strike may still be called off at the last minute
C) Ask the striking workers to make an exception for the project's tests
D) Cancel the commissioning tests and wait for the next available date
= A
? External events are assessed for impact, alternatives are prepared and stakeholders are kept informed.

@@ BE8 | Hybrid
Climate data shows that the planting season in the target region now starts two weeks earlier than it did a decade ago. What should the project do?
A) Update the product's assumptions and the timing of releases and installations
B) Keep the original timing, since the seasons may return to their old pattern in future years
C) Ask farmers to plant later so that they match the project's release timing
D) Record the data in the lessons learned register for future projects only
= A
? Changes in the physical environment can invalidate planning assumptions. The project updates its assumptions and plans to stay aligned with users' needs.

@@ BE8 | Hybrid
Half of the rail maintenance workforce will retire within five years. How should the project respond?
A) Reflect it in the design and transition plans, for example through automation and training new staff
B) Ignore it, since workforce planning belongs to the rail operator after handover
C) Ask the retiring staff to postpone their retirement until the project ends
D) Design the system for the current workforce, since the current staff know the railway best of all
= A
? Social and demographic trends affect how a solution will be operated. Considering them in design and transition planning protects long-term value.
